5973 opencode run --model opencode/deepseek-v4.1-flash-free --dir /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue --agent build --title rbops-phase-097 # DO THIS, NOW. No orientation phase.  Your working directory is the project checkout. It is the ONLY place you may read or write. The RBOPS pipeline that invoked you lives elsewhere; do not inspect it, do not audit it, and do not spend a single tool call on it.  You have one job, described below. Work in this order and do not deviate:  1. Reproduce the finding. Smallest command that shows it. Then STOP and note it. 2. Write ONE failing test that demonstrates it. Run ONLY your targeted test: `cargo test <test_name>` (fast). Do NOT run `cargo test --all-targets` during iteration — the full suite runs 67 binaries, takes 15 minutes, and will burn your time budget. Watch your test fail. 3. Make the smallest change that turns that test green. Re-run `cargo test <test_name>`. 4. Add the remaining edge-case tests required below. Test each with `cargo test <test_name>`. 5. Only now run the four gates (full suite at the end):    - cargo fmt --all -- --check    - cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    - cargo test --all-targets    - ../rbops/verify.sh "phase-097" 6. Write REPORT.md.  Budget: reproduce in 2 minutes, first test by 5, first edit by 10. If you still have not written code by then, you have misread the task — re-read this message. Do not browse. Do not read files you have not been told to read. Do not write a plan document. There is no human to review a plan.  Files: read and write ONLY inside your working directory. Never write to /tmp, /home, or anywhere outside the project — the permission system auto-rejects those calls, and in this pipeline a rejected tool call ends your run. If you need scratch space, use ./target/tmp/ inside the project.  When you are done, say DONE and stop.  ---  ## Contract you must obey (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md — RBOPS (Redblue Autonomous Ops)  This file is the contract for every agent that RBOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-097  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-097 — Parse and format dates from text, the way time.format cannot today  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `major` | | Risk | `medium` | | Depends on | phase-096 | | Timeout | 180 min | | Must change | `src/` |  ## You must change `src/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [src/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` phase-096 gives Redblue a date value but no way to get one from text or print one in a chosen shape. time.format takes a Unix timestamp and a format string, and time.unix parses one fixed 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' layout, so a program cannot read an ISO-8601 timestamp with a zone, cannot read the format another system emits, and cannot control the output shape. Every real program that reads data from somewhere else - a log line, a CSV column, an HTTP header - needs this, and the current single hardcoded layout is the reason it cannot. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Give the date value parsing and formatting: parse ISO-8601 including an offset and fractional seconds, format to a caller-chosen layout, and convert between the date value and a Unix timestamp. Extend the existing time.unix and time.format to route through the date value rather than remaining a separate implementation, so there is one date model in the runtime rather than two that can disagree. A malformed timestamp must raise a catchable error naming the offending characters, never a panic and never a silently wrong value.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-097    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] an ISO-8601 timestamp with a zone offset parses to a date value equal to the same instant expressed in UTC - asserted on literal values, not on round-tripping through the same code - [ ] formatting a date to a chosen layout produces the exact expected text, including padding and a zero-padded month against a single-digit month - [ ] the existing time.unix and time.format still return what they returned for the inputs they already handled, with the existing tests passing unmodified - [ ] a malformed timestamp raises a catchable Error::Runtime naming the position that failed to parse, and no input makes the parser panic - [ ] a timestamp with fractional seconds preserves the fraction, and one without a zone is interpreted under the stance phase-096 documented - [ ] the tree-walker and the bytecode VM parse and format identically, compared as data across both engines - [ ] SPEC.md documents the accepted layouts, including what is NOT accepted, so the limits are explicit - [ ] edge_* tests cover Feb 29 in a leap year, a leap second refused rather than accepted silently, an empty string, a date-only string, a timezone-less string, and a string with trailing garbage  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## CONTINUATION MODE A previous attempt was interrupted and its partial work is already in the tree. DO NOT restart. Inspect what exists, continue from it, finish the phase.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-097/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 097 — Parse and format dates from text  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | `src/runtime.rs` | +503 −42 | one ISO-8601 parser, date formatting, `time.parse`, date-aware `time.format` and `time.unix`, nanosecond property, bounded layout validation, a comma decimal mark in the fraction, a fractional `time.unix` answer whose window edge is checked as an instant, a floor for numeric instants, a near-one clamp, a legacy-layout fallback, and a year-aware refusal; JSON dates now use the shared text form | | `src/value.rs` | +13 −1 | one `date_text` printer shared by `say`, `to_text` and JSON, so a parsed fraction survives a round trip instead of being dropped | | `src/stdlib.rs` | +9 −2 | register `time.parse`, add the `time.parse` member, name and signature, widen the format/unix signatures | | `SPEC.md` | +52 −12 | document accepted and refused ISO-8601 layouts, date formatting, offset handling, the nanosecond property, fraction-preserving date text, fractional Unix answers, the floor of a numeric instant, the legacy-layout fallback, and the window's final-second rule | | `README.md` | +2 −1 | time quick reference names `time.parse` and a date argument to `time.format` | | `SURVEY.md` | +24 −27 | rank 6 closed, inventory 85 builtins and 63 dotted members, citations re-anchored to the current runtime lines | | `tests/time_builtin_test.rs` | +527 −5 | twenty-five tests for parsing, formatting, conversion, refusals, fraction-preserving text, fractional timestamps, legacy-layout compatibility and cross-engine agreement | | `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | +1 | names phase-097's delivery subject in the rule that acknowledges the in-flight phase's commit | | `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs` | +5 | an answerable-program row for `time.parse` | | `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt` | +4 −4 | time and date documentation blocks re-anchored after the SPEC edits |  The ISO-8601 parser, `time.parse`, the date-aware `time.format`/`time.unix` and their tests were merged by earlier attempts. A resumed run shared one `date_text` printer so a parsed fraction survives `say`/`to_text`/JSON. This resumed run finished the other conversion direction: `time.unix` dropped the fraction the date value held, so a fractional date now answers a fractional timestamp, and the final second of the shared window is refused when it carries a fraction rather than yielding a number `time.format` will not take. Three new tests pin both changes. No working code was removed.  Review round 1 fixed the conversion edges those changes exposed. Numeric instants now floor rather than truncate toward zero, so `-0.5` is 1969-12-31 23:59:59 and `time.from_unix(time.unix(pre_epoch_fraction))` names the date's own second. A fraction within half an ulp of 1 is clamped below the next second, so `.999999999` at 2024 and at the window edge cannot name the following second. `time.unix` falls back to the old chrono layout for unpadded fields and the leap second, while `time.parse` stays strict ISO. An out-of-range year is refused at the year field, and the SURVEY citations were re-anchored. Four tests pin these fixes, and the existing truncation test was updated to the floor it now names.  The ISO-8601 parser, `time.parse`, the date-aware `time.format`/`time.unix` and their tests were merged by the previous attempt. This resumed run added the one missing round-trip property they left behind: the fraction a parse kept was dropped by `say`/`to_text`/JSON, so this run shares one `date_text` printer and pins it with four new tests. No working code was removed.  ## Behaviour `time.parse(text)` reads ISO-8601 into a date value. It accepts a date only (`2024-02-29`), a date and time with `T` or a space, one to nine fractional digits, and an optional `Z` or `±HH:MM` offset. An offset is applied, so `2024-01-01T12:00:00+02:00` is the literal date `2024-01-01 10:00:00`. A text without a zone is read as UTC, the timezone stance phase-096 already documented. The fraction is kept in the date value, is readable as `nanosecond`, and is written by `say`, `to_text` and JSON: `to_text(time.parse(...))` now carries the fraction with three, six or nine digits, so the text round-trips through `time.parse`. A date with no fraction keeps the text it always had.  `time.format` now accepts a date value as well as the numeric timestamp it always accepted, and still uses `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S` when no layout is given. The numeric form returns exactly what it did before, including the UTC zone that `%Z`, `%z`, and `%+` render. `time.unix` now accepts a date value or any text `time.parse` accepts, and reads the old `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` layout through a compatibility fallback to chrono's `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`, so unpadded month, day, hour, minute and second fields and the leap second `:60` keep the answers old callers had; `time.parse` remains strict ISO and refuses both. The ISO parser also keeps the one-to-three-digit years the old `%Y` path read. A date that holds a fractional second answers a fractional number of seconds — `time.unix(time.parse("2024-02-29T23:59:58.5Z"))` is `1709251198.5` — so the timestamp, the date value and `time.format` name the same instant. A numeric instant is read at its floor, not truncated toward zero, so `time.format(-0.5)` and `time.from_unix(-0.5)` both name `1969-12-31 23:59:59`, the second the date `1969-12-31T23:59:59.5Z` falls in. A fraction that would round up to the next whole second at `f64` magnitude is clamped below it, so the number never names a second after the date value, even at the window's final second. The window ends exactly at `10000-01-01T00:00:00Z`; a fraction in that final second is past the end and is a catchable `time.unix` error.  Malformed text is a catchable `Runtime` error naming the builtin and the byte position where the expected form stopped matching, with the offending characters quoted. A leap second (`:60`), an invalid day, an out-of-range zone offset, a missing seconds field, a fraction with no digits or more than nine, and trailing garbage are refused; none reaches a panic. A layout `chrono` cannot read is refused the same way rather than panicking in its display implementation.  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | `edge_time_parse_accepts_iso_offsets_against_literal_utc_dates` | offset conversion against literal calendar values | | `edge_time_parse_keeps_fractions_and_reads_a_missing_zone_as_utc` | fraction preservation, date-only, timezone-less | | `edge_time_format_of_a_date_pads_and_honours_the_chosen_layout` | zero padding, chosen layout, default layout, unreadable and parse-only layout refusal | | `edge_time_unix_reads_a_date_value_and_iso_text_with_an_offset` | date-to-timestamp conversion, old layout preserved, pre-epoch boundary | | `edge_time_parse_refuses_malformed_text_with_a_position` | empty, malformed, leap second, trailing garbage, unicode, wrong type, catchability | | `edge_the_positive_window_boundary_round_trips_through_text` | expanded-year `+10000` through `to_text`, `time.unix`, `time.parse`, and `time.format` | | `edge_time_parse_refuses_each_out_of_range_field_at_its_position` | month 13/00, day 32, hour 24, minute 60, offset minute 60, each with its position and catchability | | `edge_time_parse_keeps_leap_days_and_refuses_leap_seconds` | 29 February in leap and common years, leap-second refusal | | `edge_the_tree_walker_and_the_bytecode_vm_agree_on_parsing_and_formatting_dates` | cross-engine data and cross-engine refusal | | `edge_time_parse_accepts_a_comma_fraction_mark_like_iso_8601` | decimal comma, nanosecond preservation, formatter agreement | | `edge_time_format_of_a_numeric_timestamp_keeps_its_utc_zone` | numeric `%Z` and `%z` render UTC rather than an empty zone | | `edge_time_unix_reads_the_short_years_the_old_layout_accepted` | one-to-three-digit years keep the answers chrono's `%Y` gave | | `edge_time_parse_reads_a_space_separator_and_a_date_only_as_utc` | space separator, timezone-less date and midnight | | `edge_time_parse_refuses_a_lone_fraction_mark_and_an_offset_without_minutes` | malformed input at an exact position, catchability | | `edge_time_unix_refuses_malformed_text_and_wrong_types` | failure channel for malformed text and wrong argument kinds | | `edge_time_unix_preserves_a_parsed_fraction` | fraction preservation through `time.unix`, binary-exact literals, pre-epoch negative fraction, offset conversion, whole second unchanged | | `edge_time_unix_fraction_agrees_across_engines_and_text` | cross-engine fractional timestamp, text round trip, formatter agreement | | `edge_time_unix_refuses_a_fraction_past_the_positive_window_edge` | out-of-bounds instant at the window's final second, catchability, whole boundary second still accepted | | `edge_date_text_keeps_a_parsed_fraction_and_round_trips` | fraction preservation through `to_text` and JSON, zero-fraction boundary, `time.parse` round trip | | `edge_date_text_uses_the_shortest_fraction_without_losing_precision` | numeric boundary: three, six and nine fraction digits, zero fraction writes no mark | | `edge_date_text_round_trip_refuses_trailing_fraction_text` | malformed input: a second fraction is refused at position 23 and is catchable | | `edge_the_tree_walker_and_the_bytecode_vm_agree_on_fractional_date_text` | cross-engine text, JSON and nanosecond agreement for a fractional date |  ## Mandatory edge-case matrix | Row | Status | |---|---| | empty | covered — `time.parse("")` is refused at position 0 | | singleton | N/A — a timestamp is fixed-field text, not a collection with a singleton case | | boundary | covered — leap day 2024/2000, common-year 29 February, epoch `-1`, offset conversion, positive window boundary `+10000`, and a fraction in its final second refused | | out_of_bounds | covered — 30 February, month 13/00, day 32, hour 24, minute 60, offset hour/minute 24/60, more than nine fraction digits, out-of-window Unix seconds, and a fraction past the window edge | | type_mismatch | covered — `time.parse(5)`, a list, no argument, a numeric layout, and a date where a timestamp was expected | | numeric_boundary | covered — one to nine fraction digits after either mark, 1900 versus 2000 leap rules, pre-epoch fractional timestamp `-0.5`, shared Unix window | | unicode | covered — trailing CJK text and full-width digits are refused with a position and no panic | | nesting_recursion | N/A — the parser is an iterative fixed field walk with no recursion | | duplicate_missing_keys | N/A — timestamps have fixed fields and no user-supplied keys | | malformed_input | covered — empty text, bad day, missing seconds, empty/oversized fraction, a decimal mark with no digits, an offset without minutes, bad offset, trailing garbage | | resource_limit | N/A — parsing is single-pass over the input, allocates only the error snippet, and has no loop guard or recursion limit to set |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass | | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass | | `cargo test --all-targets` | 77 test binaries, 2273 passed, 0 failed | | `../rbops/verify.sh phase-097` | pass |  ## Invariants touched - `time.format` and `time.unix` gain an additional accepted argument kind (a   date value); their existing numeric and text behaviour is unchanged. - `time.unix` accepts the ISO-8601 text `time.parse` accepts, a superset of the   old fixed layout, so old callers keep their answers. - A date property `nanosecond` is added; existing date properties are unchanged. - A date's printed text now keeps a non-zero fraction. Dates without a fraction   print exactly as before, so every existing `say`/`to_text`/JSON answer for   them is unchanged. - `time.unix` now answers a fractional number for a date that holds a fraction.   Whole-second dates answer exactly the integer they did before, so every   pre-existing input keeps its answer. - The list of date values, `.rb` extension, grammar, and error kinds are   unchanged.  ## Existing test reconciliation - `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt` re-anchors the dates block to `SPEC.md:1448` and   the regex block to `SPEC.md:1555`. Both moved again because the time   documentation gained the fractional-timestamp paragraph; both were executed   by the suite, not merely listed. - `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` adds `resume phase-097:` to   `DELIVERY_SUBJECT_PREFIXES`, because the resumed delivery commit is the   phase's own and cannot be pinned by SHA. - `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs` gains the `time_parse` answerable row that   the registry-coverage test requires for every registered builtin. - `SURVEY.md` inventory now reads 85 builtins and 63 dotted members; rank 6 keeps   its historical blocked count with "0 since phase-097" so the ranking still   descends as its own test requires.  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - `time.parse` does not take a caller-chosen input layout; it accepts the ISO   shapes documented in `SPEC.md` and refuses everything else. A layout-based   parser is a separate capability and is not needed for the ISO-8601 finding. - A date value is timezone-naive, so the offset a parsed text carried is applied   and then forgotten. The instant is correct, but printing it back cannot name   the original offset. - `time.parse` does not enforce the `time.format` numeric window; a date outside   it can be constructed, but `time.unix` refuses to convert it, as documented.   A fraction in the window's final second is refused for the same reason. - `time.format` of a numeric timestamp still truncates a fractional second, the   behaviour it had before this phase; only a date value carries the fraction   into a layout.  ```
